<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Legends of Panama</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> My Favorite Legend</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Present their views on simple issues. Interactive: Respond to classmates' presentations.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can present views on topics within the legend (e.g., "The hero was brave because he helped others."). Can respond to classmates' ideas about Panamanian legends and ask follow-up questions (e.g., "I agree with you. La Tulivieja was terrifying. What would you have done if you had heard her crying by the river at night?"). Can describe short stories or legends to peers (e.g., "There once was a clever rabbit.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of characters or scenes from well-known Panamanian legends (e.g., La Tulivieja, El Chivato, La Niña Encantada del Salto del Pilón) that were discussed in previous lessons. Students work in pairs and verbally answer: "Which legend is this? What was happening in this picture?" followed by "What was your favorite legend we discussed last week, and why?" Students share one idea per pair with the class, activating prior knowledge and encouraging initial speaking.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models expressing an opinion and describing events using past simple and past continuous. For example, "Last week, we read about La Tulivieja. I think she was a terrifying creature. She was always crying by the river at night. The villagers were often frightened when they heard her." The teacher exaggerates pronunciation of -ed endings (/t/, /d/, /ɪd/) and -ing endings. The teacher also uses sequence markers like 'often' and 'at night'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was La Tulivieja a brave hero or a terrifying spirit?" (Terrifying spirit)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was she doing by the river?" (She was crying.) "When was she doing this?" (At night, often.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Legends of Panama".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short (1.5-2 minute) audio recording or teacher-narrated story of a lesser-known Panamanian legend (e.g., "El Chivato") focusing on narrative events, character descriptions, and actions. The story includes target vocabulary (e.g., <i>monster, hero, village, river, explore, encounter, fight, bravely, suddenly, in the end</i>) and grammatical structures (past simple, past continuous).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describe short stories or legends to peers. Tell a story and sequence events.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Listening for gist and detail, preparing for speaking):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays/narrates the legend. Students listen and identify the main character (hero/monster) and setting. "Who was the main character in this story? Where was the story happening?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Second listening: Students listen for key events. "What was the brave hero doing when he encountered the creature?" (Focus on past continuous). "What happened suddenly?" (Focus on adverbs of time).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In pairs, students orally retell 2-3 main events from the story using sequence markers like "first," "then," "in the end."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Narrating past events using past simple and past continuous, describing characters and actions in legends, employing adverbs of time and sequence markers. Practice pronunciation of -ed and -ing endings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion (Oral):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides sentence stems on the board or a handout related to a legend (e.g., "Once upon a time, a _______ (brave/evil) _______ (hero/witch) _______ (live) in a _______ (village/mountain). One day, while he/she _______ (explore) the _______ (river/town), he/she _______ (encounter) a _______ (mysterious/strong) _______ (creature/spirit)."). Students verbally complete the sentences, choosing words from a given list (target vocabulary) and conjugating verbs in past simple or continuous. They then read their completed sentences aloud to their partner, focusing on correct verb forms and -ed, -ing pronunciation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Picture Storytelling:</b> The teacher displays a sequence of 3-4 pictures depicting key moments from a simple, familiar legend. In pairs, students take turns describing what is happening in each picture, using past continuous for ongoing actions and past simple for completed events. They must use at least three target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>monster, fight, save, bravely, suddenly</i>) and sequence markers (<i>first, then, after</i>). Example: "First, the monster was sleeping. Then, a brave hero appeared."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Story Chain:</b> Students form groups of three. One student starts a sentence about a legend, using past continuous (e.g., "Long ago, a ghost was hiding in an old house..."). The next student adds a sentence, continuing the story and using past simple or an adverb of time (e.g., "...and suddenly, a curious girl entered the house."). The third student adds another sentence, using vocabulary and sequence markers (e.g., "The girl was exploring the rooms, and she found a hidden treasure."). They continue for 3-4 rounds, creating a short, collaborative spoken narrative.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Legends of Panama".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> "My Favorite Panamanian Legend: Peer Presentation and Discussion."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Informal small-group presentations followed by interactive Q&A.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Individual Preparation (3 min):</b> Each student thinks about their favorite Panamanian legend (or one they know well). They make quick mental notes or bullet points about: <i>Who were the main characters (hero, monster, spirit, witch)? Where did the story take place (village, river, mountain)? What was happening (fight, explore, save, rescue)? What happened first, next, and in the end?</i> They should plan to use past simple and continuous, sequence markers, and at least 5 target vocabulary words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Small Group Presentations (8-10 min):</b> Students form groups of 3-4. Each student takes turns presenting their chosen legend (approx. 1-1.5 minutes per student). They should describe the story clearly and express their views on why it's their favorite. Example opening: "My favorite legend is La Tulivieja. Once upon a time, she was a beautiful woman, but one day, she transformed into a terrifying creature. She was always crying by the river at night, and her screams were very loud."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interactive Q&A:</b> After each presentation, the other group members must ask at least one follow-up question or express an opinion about the legend. Examples: "I agree with you! La Tulivieja was terrifying. What would you have done if you had heard her crying by the river at night?" or "The hero was very brave. How did he save the village?" The teacher circulates, observing, prompting, and providing support for interaction.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">11-13 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on speaking output and peer assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' "My Favorite Panamanian Legend" presentations and their participation in the follow-up Q&A within their small groups.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist & Peer Feedback Sheet):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Clear articulation, especially of -ed (/t/, /d/, /ɪd/) and -ing endings.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Incorporates a range of words related to legends (e.g., <i>legend, monster, hero, village, fight, save, brave, mysterious, suddenly</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Accuracy:</b> Correct use of Past Simple and Past Continuous to narrate events.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Coherence:</b> Describes the legend clearly and sequences events logically using markers (<i>once upon a time, first, then, after, in the end</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interactive Response:</b> Ability to ask relevant follow-up questions and respond to classmates' ideas/questions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket (Oral or Quick Write):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word or phrase you successfully used today to talk about legends?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one thing you did well when telling your legend, and one thing you want to practice more?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt (Whole class discussion or pair share):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel now about describing a legend to someone else in English? What part of speaking about legends was most enjoyable for you today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about using the past continuous and past simple together, and how can you improve?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully present their views on simple issues and respond to classmates' presentations? Were the learning outcomes met?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific vocabulary or grammatical structures (e.g., past continuous, -ed endings) need further reinforcement based on observed errors or hesitation during speaking activities?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How effectively did the interactive tasks promote student-to-student communication? Were all students engaged, or do some require more scaffolding for participation?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "Great job speaking about Panamanian legends today! You used many new words and told exciting stories. In our next lesson (Lesson 4), we will take these same ideas and vocabulary to write a short paragraph about 'My Favorite Legend,' focusing on organizing our thoughts clearly in written form. This will help us remember the stories and practice sequencing events."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>